import crate::pattern_text::pattern_text
import redox_ast::Pattern

-- | One pattern in a position whose next token is a **keyword or an arrow**,
-- | rather than another pattern.
-- |
-- | The two such positions: the inner pattern of an `@` binding, and an
-- | alternative of an `or`. Nothing needs parentheses there except a nested
-- | `or` itself, and the reason is worth stating because the first version of
-- | this leaf got it wrong in both places:
-- |
-- | `pattern_argument`'s rule — parenthesise anything that could absorb what
-- | follows — is right for a **constructor argument**, where what follows is
-- | another pattern and juxtaposition is greedy. After `@` and after `or`, what
-- | follows is `or`, `->` or `,`, and a pattern cannot absorb any of those:
-- | they are keywords and punctuation, not arguments. So the two positions take
-- | different predicates, and `whole @ Rect { w, h }` and `Some 1 or None` —
-- | both written exactly that way in `examples/` — came out as
-- | `whole @ (Rect { w, h })` and `(Some 1) or None` until they did.
-- |
-- | A nested `or` is the one exception, because `or` *is* one of the tokens that
-- | follows, so an alternative that is itself an alternation would be read at
-- | the outer level.
export(crate) beside_a_keyword : &Pattern -> String
beside_a_keyword pattern = case pattern of
  Pattern::Or _ -> format! \-> "(#{(pattern_text pattern)})"
  other -> pattern_text other
